From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: form, space and order

>Peter McCluskey saith:
>> My ideal virtual world would be at least 4D,

Geometers generally agree that four-space has the most interesting
geometry, followed closely by three-space.  All the other dimensions have
simpler geometry.  So being able to visualize five or more dimensions
probably doesn't pay off much more than being able to visualize four. 
Alas, we are stuck with our primitive monkey brains, only able to visualize
three.

Dasher writes:
>
>One potential advantage of 3-space is that some vector operations
>work there and nowhere else.

I presume you're talking about the "cross product".  It turns out that the
cross product works in any dimension, and actually returns a thing called a
two-form.  In 3D the difference between a vector and a two-form is covered
up by the fact that they both have three degrees of freedom, and can thus
both be written as vectors.  This is endlessly confusing if you try to do
tensor calculus in 3-space, because two-forms don't transform like vectors.
 In any other dimension, The difference between a vector and a two-form is
clear, and tensor calculus gets much easier to understand.

>
>Do 4D animals have three eyes?

Nope, all they need is two.  It's the angular difference in the direction
of the two eyes that tells you how far away something is.


