Subject: Thornes for Criticism

One of the "Decline and Fall" kvetches I've made is the lack of feedback
I get on my posts.  I get e-mail responses to only a fraction of my
posts, and then usually from the same few people (who my thanks go
out to!).  I don't know if anybody else even reads the stuff I 
write, much less what they think of it.  This is probably a situation
common to many posters, as I've seen others complaining
about this.  To quote Tim May's sig, "I've put time and money into this
post, I hope you enjoy it."  To which I would add, if you don't enjoy
it I'd love to know that also.  We've talked about n-dimensional voting, 
etc. Such schemes might help alleviate the feedback problem as some
point in the future, but they are nontrivial to implement, and involve 
very lossy compression from a sophisticated opinion down to to a number.

One of the things I've learned over the years is that criticism is
as valuable as creativity; in fact it's an indispensible part of 
creativity.  So perhaps I'm just whining to expect it for free (despite
the fact that I post for free).  So I will kvetch no longer,
but instead shell out good hard (well, soft) Thornes for private 
criticism of my essays.  I will pay p100 (worth between $.10 and
$1.00, depending on whether you judge by the bid or ask price)
for any criticisms of any essay I write on any forum ("essay"
defined as anything with over 1 page of original material from me).
These critiques must meet the following minimum criteria:

* Between a half page and two pages of original material.
* Mailed to me only.
* In good grammatical English.

I will commonly tack on bonuses, ranging between p100 and p500,
based on my own discretion.   The largest bonuses will be given for 
factual refutation or confirmation of my claims.  I will tend to
favor negative feedback over positive feedback.  Criteria and prices 
subject to future change based on supply and demand.  All criticisms 
will be treated as private e-mail and held in confidence.

Send your critisms with your e-mail address (as recognized by HEx)
to me at szabo@netcom.com.   I look forward to reading what you
have to say!

