[I wrote this 3 weeks ago, and have been sitting on it. A couple
of recent events have prompted me send it. 1) The death of my friend
and colleague, Rob Wolff (a specialist in scientific computing at 
Apple Computer), last Thursday, shook me up, pointing out how fragile 
life can be, how important it is to speak up and take risks, and it 
made me want to work harder so that Rob's dreams don't die with him, and 
2) Kennita's "love and relationships" post. She and I are somewhat on 
parallel tracks, one difference being that she is considering the 
future, where I'm considering extropian relevances to the present, 
and how to get from *here* to *there*. --AG] 


Dear Extropians:

A number of times the question has come up on why there are so few
women participating on this list.  I've been observing these list procedings 
for about two years, and so I hope I have gained enough insight to address 
that question. 

I'm speaking mainly about the women who are already here, which, admittedly,
are damn few. However, my suggestions below could be extended and used in 
daily conversations with female acquaintenances and family members. Here 
are tips for extropian recruiters, perhaps?

My reasons for not posting very much are (in order of importance),
lack of time, lack of knowledge (I'm a physicist and programmer, and
even so, many of these topics are new to me.), and lack of interest.
The following essay will address that lack of interest.

Extropianism, as promoted by a number of people on this list, is
is a philosophy of TRANSHumanism.  That is, BEYOND humanism.  However to
get from here to there, one would want to apply Extropian ideas to the
current humanistic realm as well. I think that's where you may be losing
the interest and participation of many of the female members. I suspect
that some of the gentlemen on this list would be interested to discuss
humanistic topics as well, and we could use some fresh topics on this
list....

The current extropian philosophy as promoted by More et al, seems
quite bleak and barren to me. It could use a little filling out.

What follows, is, of course, my personal opinion, I am not speaking for 
all of my gender.

First and foremost, I see a large lack of awareness and interest about
topics that many women are concerned with today. This isn't a problem,
except that you might be missing an opportunity to think and talk about 
how extropian ideas might be relevant to helping women as well as men.

	* A recent example: Most women's difficulty to both succeed in
	a career and raise a child.  I can't think of a single issue
	more important to a woman in her 20's and 30's who is very
	actively persuing her interests and finding she's successful.
	I found it ironic that that the topic discussed, when I was
	asking about freezing human eggs, was "startup businesses", 
	a topic that many woman can only fantasize and dream about
	because they barely have enough time to work and keep their 
	families going (or begin one), much less devote energy and long 
	hours to a startup business.  Let's see, 340 subscribers, 90% male, 
	say 50% of those are in long-term relationships.  Out of ~150 men, 
	only a two or three, here, ever thought about this issue?

	Let me spell out the _ramifications_ of freezing eggs as a way
	to postpone child-bearing. Young 20ish-age couples who postponed
	starting a family now have more money and more time to devote to
	their careers.  A large number of women who have one goal in mind-
	their careers- will be flooding the marketplace. Companies catering to 
	egg donors start a thriving industry (another niche for Alcor, 
	perhaps?). Businesses will now have to accomodate older, more 
	established, employees in their 40's as they begin their new families.
	These same companies will find that their younger employees are now 
	more educated than they were before. Congresscritters will pass new 
	laws to try to get their hands on the money being made in the new 
	markets that spring up.  Women wanting the old traditional role will 
	create a backlash against those who want to freeze their eggs.  I see 
	the social ramifications as greater than that caused by women's
	birth control pill. Jeesh! I can't believe all of you missed this!

	Suggestions:
	Some discusssions along the lines of what I outlined above.
	Keeping track of new medical breakthroughs in fertility, in-vitero 
	fertilization.  Surrogate mothers. How to apply PPL to the multitude
	of different reproduction methods. Free market home schools. New styles
	of working to accomodate career mothers. Wiring up your home for 
	telecommunication. What technical or scientific jobs can be carried 
	out at home? Ways to beat the IRS for small businesses.

	
	* Health issues. We've had lots of discussion to make one smarter. 
	Lots of discussion about woman's birth control.  Lots of discussion
	about conscious-altering chemical substances. BUT....

	Suggestions:
	How about discussions about the studies of MALE contraceptives?  That
	handy little extropian device called the condom might prove to
	be an interesting topic (what is your favorite condom?). How about
	discussions about drugs to increase one's endurance? Increasing one's
	strength? Do psychedelics have an affect on a baby in the womb? What 
	nutritive substances can eleviate bladder or yeast infections? 
	(Common female maladies) Nutritive substances to enhance sexual 
	drive?  Now what *really* is going on in tantric sex?

	* Communication issues. Are you aware of the difficulties many
	women face in stating needs and wants and criticisms in a job envi-
	ronment, esp. one in a technical field? I sometimes write memos
	to my various bosses about resources I need to acquire to complete
	my job. If I state these lack of items in a simple and matter-of-fact 
	way, I'm perceived as coming on too strong, and bosses get mad.  If I
	state it gently, and inviting discussion, I'm not taken seriously
	and ignored.  There's a middle ground that many women are always
	trying to find in communicating with their bosses and co-workers,
	who are male. I've observed that with the same direct and factual memo
	coming from a male and a female- the male is perceived as strong
	and taking initiative while the female is perceived as being pushy.

	Suggestions:
	Discussions for what works and what doesn't work in approaches for 
	conveying information. Relevant psychology. How does electronic
	communication help and hurt in the conveying of information? Memes
	that are easily transmitted and facillitate communication between 
	sexes? So men, what communication approaches cause *you* to listen? 
	And please don't tell me to go read _You Don't Understand_ by Tannen.  
	I found this book very damaging in propagating the male-female 
	stereotypes.

	*Interpersonal and relationship issues. Why hasn't the "sacred" topic
	of marriage ever come up on this list? Why hasn't interpersonnal 
	contracts like cohabitation contracts ever been discussed?

	Suggestions: Given the horrendous baggage with church and state (and 
	IRS), I would like to see some discussions on why some extropians 
	got married. How does one in daily life handle community property and 
	separate property in such a union? What methods did you use to avoid
	the religious aspect (or did you?)? Isn't it bothersome that when you
	get a credit check, your mate's credit is automatically checked too? 
	What kind of cohabitation contracts (if any) have people here invoked?
	Did it help or hurt to have such an agreement? Is polygomy preferred?
	Monogamy?


Second, A lack of humor on this list.  I miss Tom Morrow's posts a lot. This
list is probably just a bit too dry for many women's tastes.  (I'll have to 
post a wonderful somewhat dirty joke that I just heard yesterday.) Haven't you
noticed how a good belly laugh lightens, reorders, reasserts power and 
strength,  and eases depression?  A writer I admire said that laughter makes 
people glad they are alive, happy to be here, more conscious of love, 
heightened with  eros, and it severs them from anger.  Laughter makes a person
healthier and better.


	Suggestions: 
	Post more jokes. Be playful. Try to see the humor in what
	might otherwise be perceived as a nasty message. (Could alleviate some 
	flamewars.) And a reminder: the Mistress Romana personna was started 
	to inject some humor into the list- I've noticed that many here are 
	actually taking her characature seriously. 

	[Digression.. If you guys insist on pushing this "E-babe" thingy,
	I would like to, in turn, push an "E-stud" thingy.  Heehee.  I'll 
	have to think of someone who could, you know, look good in one of
	those Jockey briefs ads. Hmmm.. Time to draw on my many hours of 
	experience of gazing at naked extropian male bodies in hot tubs!]
	

Third, A lack of the arts and the aesthetic realm.  Sure we're all technical 
people here and that is our jobs and interests. But presumably you have other 
interests related to the arts as well? How do you hope to integrate that into 
the extropian future?  An extropian world without the arts doesn't sound like 
much fun to me.

		
	Suggestions:
	Discussions about mapping the senses in simulated environments. What
	do you use to heighten any of the five senses? What turns you on and
	what doesn't turn you on, and if its a collective phenomenon, how do 
	you think that might affect human's evolution during the next several 
	thousand years? How can the arts be used to convey free-market ideas?
	Have you experimented with how music affects your moods? Has anyone 
	else, besides me, noticed that comic books often express anti-
	authority, individualistic, fun and funny views on humankind's future?
	What are your favorite comic books? If extropian interractions were 
	mapped into a complex expression on a dance floor- what would that 
	look like?  Let's see... Perry would be slam-dancing. Harry would be 
	performing a ballroom dance. Romana would be doing a tango. Max More 
	would be stomping his feet flamenco-style. Tim May might be whooping 
	a war chant around a sacrificial fire. Well you get the idea.


Finally, I want to say that the level of sexist remarks here is very low or
nonexistent, and I appreciate that.  Please keep it up. You will lose some 
female readers if sexist remarks becomes commonplace. (I almost unsubscribed 
1 1/2 years ago over a  very callous and assinine remark by a well-known 
poster when he commented about his pregnant wife acting like a wet dish rag. 
I decided to boycott his books instead.)


Thank you for listening.

