Subject: VirtSem: VirtBiz

Nick Szabo:

> I posit the following: real-time interaction is not as important 
> in virtual worlds.  When people are in virtual worlds they usually
> like to dispense with real-time constraints and deal with the world as
> if it were a "to do" list.  This is why, for example, e-mail is so much 
> more popular and effective than "talk".

I think a large part of this is just what can be accomplished with
various technologies.  People like real-time interaction for some
things and non-real-time interaction for other things.  Currently,
Net-based communication methods favor those things that aren't
real-time.  If I can type at 40 WPM then when a keyboard is my
only output device to a communication medium I can't interact
with somebody else at greater than 32 baud.  Anybody who's used
talk or IRC knows that can be maddening for truly interactive
communication, which works best with a much higher bandwidth.
If it takes me fifteen minutes to write these thoughts, it's a
good thing that I can do it offline and the people who might
read this don't have to sit and watch me do it -- thus e-mail,
traditional books, and so on.

I've seen a few people comment that hypertext-style interaction
a la html might improve the quality of an interactive virtual
environment, but I just don't buy it.  All that does is increase
the bandwidth in one direction; maybe I can do a little better
with a mouse, but not much.  I'm reminded of Captain Pike from
the old Star Trek program, who was only able to beep yes and no
at the world for maybe a 1-baud outgoing link for interactivity.
In FTF interactions, we project a great deal more even than
the text transcript of our words because we're highly skilled
in forming judgements about individuals based on subtle cues
about body language and tone of voice.  I actually talked to
Nick on the telephone a while ago -- quite an interesting
experience!

When I think about "interaction", this is what I think of,
rather than the amount of control I have over what information
I can receive; is a book interactive because I can turn
the pages at will and use the index?  Is hypertext interactive
because I can choose the order of presentation of canned text?
Not to me it isn't.  To me the most sophisticated MUD in the
world won't be an appreciably more interactive medium until
we can effective crank up the user -> system bandwidth; increasing
the bandwidth of the reverse direction may enhance the experience,
but not the interactivity.

As the technology improves, we will of course be able to use
the same interactive techniques we are so highly trained in
right now, but it's much more interesting than this because
we won't be constrained by only emitting unprocessed photons
and compression waves from our physical bodies.  The interesting
question to me is how to make better use of the *outgoing* data
channel.

Any thoughts on how to do this?  I can only think of adaptive
mulit-dimensional input devices that we could use to manipulate
flexible but well-defined *structured* information spaces, but
now I'm starting to sound (sound?) like Jaron Lanier, so I'll stop.

derek
