Date: Mon, 6 Dec 93 13:34:11 EST
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Love and Relationship in the Transhuman Age

I sure hope touch, bodies and nature survive for a couple hundred
subjective years at least.

The main things that give me deep satisfaction are nature (large,
plain, complex and self-created), good intellectual stuff 
(people who have fun with ideas, and connection to that giant
background of ideas), and people.  People exist on both sides of
that spectrum and also account for the good stuff in the middle--
family, friendship, conversation, sex, work...

Some of those warm fuzzies carry across on the net (as it is--
the present internet isn't necessarily a good image of how it
could be).  Usually I get them from people with a combination of
culture, schmartz and brevity.  Most of the people I
like that way I also like in person.  There's another crossover
effect--with email I get to say things and take on roles I
wouldn't try otherwise...and then when I see people in person I
have to fill in for the me who said those things!

I'm a culture/nature, electronic/in-person amphibian. 
Maybe that's like saying I have a left and right brain, but
my brains have never seemed independent of my guts and skin.

There are things that may be the electronic/virtual-reality/
post-singularity equivalents of the sense of reality that I
need to feel satisfaction: robustness, integrity and 
connectedness.  We don't see much robustness in
computers these days, but when I do see it, I get some of *that
feeling*.  I mean, it's not usually conscious, but a computer
with the kind of reliability that's typical today is absolutely
no place you'd want to live.  With reliability I would include
the kind of conceptual reliability where you know that there 
are no bizarre incompatibilities or incongruous transitions.
These days using a computer is like having your brain in a
blender and then the fuse blows, but once in a while you get the
feeling that things could be different and maybe a virtual
world wouldn't be as dessicated and empty as the idea usually 
seems.

The world we live in is already a construct.  Already I wonder
how much of life's complexity (incompatible realms you have to
spread yourself thinly between) is necessary.  I mean, I think 
of the way our cities and houses and jobs and communications and
products are structured as a kind of virtual reality.  Modern
life is an interface between each individual and the
rest of the world.  So, you can ask the question (already),
how *good* an interface is it?  How clear, facile, rich, etc.?

So when I imagine a utopia with extended families living in teepees 
on the plains, or cabins in the mountains, I wonder where the
computer screen and bookshelf-surrogate is going to go and
how it's all going to fit into the *real* world it's part of.

Maybe satisfying bodies and environments will remain cheap 
relative to minds to enjoy them with.  Maybe minds with rich
tastes are the only kinds that can accomplish the real
worthwhile stuff.  Maybe the civilized universe can contain
more and more stuff and yet still be locally continuous and
sensible.  Or maybe a market for simplicity can be established
that will slow the global pace to one we can all assimilate.
Or maybe not on all scores.

Connection between virtual reality and real reality is like
the connection of touching someone.

I'm not sure I answered the question!

-fnerd
but quote me anyhow



