Subject: Random Thoughts

Re: Retina tracking

This technology is several years old in the military flight simulator
business (where the image generation rates are very demanding).  The
pilot has a helmet with a camera that identifies where the eyes are
looking.  The image generation is done in high detail for the portion
of the field of view that corresponds to the part of your retina that
has high resolution, and the rest of the filed is done in lower
resolution.  The savings in computer time, hence simulator cost, were
quite substantial at the time.  At some point, the cost of more MIPS
will fall below the cost of the eyeball tracker, and it will be
cheaper to simply generate the full detail image.

